%S A226136 1,2,4,7,10,15,22,32,47,69,101,148,217,318,466,683,1001,1467,2150,
%T A226136 3151,4618,6768,9919,14537,21305,31224,45761,67066,98290,144051,
%U A226136 211117,309407,453458,664575,973982,1427440,2092015,3065997,4493437,6585452,9651449,14144886
%N A226136 Positions of the positive integers in the ordering of rational numbers as generated by the rules: 1 is in S, and if nonzero x is in S, then x+1 and -1/x are in S. (See Comments.)
%C A226136 Let S be the set of numbers defined by these rules: 1 is in S, and if nonzero x is in S, then x + 1 and -1/x are in S. Then S is the set of all rational numbers, produced in generations as follows: g(1) = (1), g(2) = (2, -1), g(3) = (3, -1/2, 0), g(4) = (4, -1/3, 1/2), ... For n > 4, once g(n-1) = (c(1), ..., c(z)) is defined, g(n) is formed from the vector (c(1)+1, -1/c(1), c(2)+1, -1/c(2), ..., c(z)+1, -1/c(z)) by deleting previously generated elements.
%C A226136 Let S' denote the sequence formed by concatenating the generations.
%C A226136   A226130: Denominators of terms of S'.
%C A226136   A226131: Numerators of terms of S'.
%C A226136   A226136: Positions of positive integers in S'.
%C A226136   A226137: Positions of integers in S'.
%H A226136 Chaz Reid, <a href="/A226136/b226136.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1000</a> (terms 1..35 from Clark Kimberling).
%H A226136 Clark Kimberling and Peter J. C. Moses, <a href="https://www.fq.math.ca/Papers1/52-5/Kimberling.pdf">The infinite Fibonacci tree and other trees generated by rules</a>, Fibonacci Quarterly 52 (2014), no. 5, 136-150. See Example 4.2, p. 141.
%H A226136 Chaz Reid, <a href="/A226136/a226136.pdf">Proof of Barker's Conjecture for A226136</a>, 2026.
%H A226136 <a href="/index/Rec#order_03">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (1,0,1).
%F A226136 Conjecture: a(n) = a(n-1)+a(n-3) for n>6. G.f.: -x*(x+1) * (x^2+1)^2 / (x^3+x-1). - _Colin Barker_, Jul 03 2013
%F A226136 From _Chaz Reid_, Oct 11 2026: (Start)
%F A226136 Conjectures confirmed (see Reid link).
%F A226136 a(n) = A003410(n) for n >= 4. This is equivalent to the g.f. above, which equals the g.f. of A003410, (1+x)*(1+x^2)/(1-x-x^3), minus (1 + x + x^2 + x^3).
%F A226136 a(n) = 1 + Sum_{k=0..n-2} A226275(k). (End)
%e A226136 S' = (1/1, 2/1, -1/1, 3/1, -1/2, 0/1, 4/1, -1/3, 1/2, ...), with positive integers appearing in positions 1,2,4,7,...
%t A226136 g[1] := {1}; z = 20; g[n_] := g[n] = DeleteCases[Flatten[Transpose[{# + 1, -1/#}]]&[DeleteCases[g[n - 1], 0]], Apply[Alternatives, Flatten[Map[g, Range[n - 1]]]]]; Flatten[Map[g, Range[7]]]  (* ordered rationals *)
%t A226136 Map[g, Range[z]]; Table[Length[g[i]], {i, 1, z}] (* cf A003410 *)
%t A226136 f = Flatten[Map[g, Range[z]]];
%t A226136 Take[Denominator[f], 100] (* A226130 *)
%t A226136 Take[Numerator[f], 100]   (* A226131 *)
%t A226136 p1 = Flatten[Table[Position[f, n], {n, 1, z}]] (* A226136 *)
%t A226136 p2 = Flatten[Table[Position[f, -n], {n, 0, z}]];
%t A226136 Union[p1, p2]  (* A226137 *) (* _Peter J. C. Moses_, May 26 2013 *)
%o A226136 (Python)
%o A226136 from functools import reduce
%o A226136 def A226136(n): return (1, 2, 4)[n-1] if n < 4 else reduce(lambda t, _: (t[1], t[2], t[2] + t[0]), range(n-4), (7, 10, 15))[0] # _Chaz Reid_, Oct 11 2026
%Y A226136 Cf. A003410, A226080 (rabbit ordering of positive rationals).
%Y A226136 Cf. A226130, A226131, A226137, A226274, A226275.
%K A226136 nonn,easy,changed
%O A226136 1,2
%A A226136 _Clark Kimberling_, May 28 2013
